export const METHODOLOGY = "# Komorebi Texas Screen — Methodology\n\n*Smart Investments, Houston · config version 2026-09-30 · methodology as of 6 October 2026*\n\nThis note documents every assumption behind the screen, so that it can be handed to a lender or a joint-venture partner without rework. All weights, thresholds and assumptions live in `pipeline/config.yaml`; the numbers below are the values in force on the date above.\n\n## 1. Purpose and thesis\n\nThe screen ranks every utility-scale photovoltaic plant in ERCOT as an acquisition target for conversion into an AI compute campus under the Komorebi cooling-first model: the array is reused as a ground-coupled cooling asset, and modular compute is placed on the plant's existing generation interconnection.\n\n**The thesis inversion.** Poor generation performance is a price signal, not a defect. A low capture rate, high curtailment, a capacity factor below the regional benchmark and older modules all make a plant cheaper to buy and score *higher*. Nothing is filtered out for performing poorly.\n\nThe screen is built from public data. Every figure is a screening estimate, not a diligence finding.\n\n## 2. Universe and hard filters\n\nThe plant master joins the **USPVDB v4.0** array polygons (USGS / LBNL, DOI 10.5066/P9IA3TUS) to **EIA-860M** generators on the EIA plant id, rolled up from generators to plants (Texas, energy source SUN, prime mover PV). EIA-860 annual adds grid voltage, tracking and module technology; EIA-923 adds annual net generation.\n\n| Filter | Rule | Result if not met |\n|---|---|---|\n| Size | AC capacity ≥ 10 MW | fail |\n| Commercial operation | first COD between 1 Jan 2015 and 31 Dec 2022 | fail |\n| Location | Texas; plants outside the ERCOT balancing authority are flagged, not dropped | flag |\n| Technology | utility-scale PV (no CSP, rooftop or community-solar aggregations) | fail |\n| Footprint | ≥ 60 acres and ≥ 5 acres per MW AC of **host-parcel** area (TxGIO parcels) | fail |\n\nThe footprint test needs site area. USPVDB measures the *array*, which runs smaller than the site, so the test is applied to the host parcels where they could be read with adequate confidence. Plants without usable parcels are judged on the array and sent to **review** (scored, not ranked) rather than failed. Nothing is dropped silently: every plant carries `filter_status` (pass / review / fail) and `filter_reasons`.\n\nAs of 5 October 2026: 243 Texas PV plants; 75 pass (10,346.8 MW AC), 1 review (250.0 MW), 167 fail.\n\n## 3. Compute envelope and tiers\n\n| Quantity | Formula | Default |\n|---|---|---|\n| DC capacity | EIA-860 DC MW; else AC × default ILR | ILR 1.30 when DC is missing |\n| Firm IT load | DC MW × 0.5 MW IT per MWdc | Komorebi: 50 MW IT per 100 MWdc |\n| Planned facility load | firm IT × PUE | PUE 1.15 (cooling-first placeholder) |\n| SB6 review | planned load ≥ 75 MW | PUCT large-load threshold (16 TAC §25.194 draft) |\n\n**Tiers** (AC MW): T3 Modular 10–25 · T2 Mid 25–75 · T1a Anchor sub-threshold 75–*sb6 line* · T1b Anchor SB6 path ≥ *sb6 line*. The T1a/T1b line is computed, not typed: 75 MW ÷ (1.15 × 0.5 × 1.30) = **100.33 MW AC**, so it moves when any of those inputs changes. The tier uses the default ILR; the SB6 flag uses each plant's own ILR, so a plant with an unusual ILR can sit on the \"wrong\" side of the line — both are shown.\n\n## 4. Data sources\n\n| Source | Used for | Vintage in this run |\n|---|---|---|\n| USPVDB v4.0 (USGS / LBNL) | array polygons, array area | April 2026 release |\n| EIA-860M | plant list, AC MW, COD, operator, balancing authority | August 2026 |\n| EIA-860 annual | grid voltage at the POI, tracking, module technology, DC MW | 2025 final |\n| EIA-923 | annual net generation, capacity factor | 2025 |\n| ERCOT CDR workbook | resource names, counties, MW, in-service year (crosswalk) | November 2026 MORA |\n| ERCOT 60-day SCED disclosure | 15-minute output, HSL and base points per PV resource | 11 Dec 2023 – 2 Aug 2026 |\n| ERCOT real-time settlement point prices | 15-minute node and hub prices | same window |\n| OpenStreetMap (Geofabrik Texas extract) | transmission lines and substations ≥ 100 kV; interstates | October 2026 |\n| TxGIO StratMap Land Parcels | host parcels, owners, acreage, appraised value | 2026 statewide |\n| Texas RRC pipeline layer | natural-gas transmission and gathering lines | October 2026 |\n| FEMA National Flood Hazard Layer | flood zones; digital-map availability | October 2026 |\n| BTS NTAD rail network; PeeringDB | Class I mainline (fiber proxy); carrier-hotel locations | October 2026 |\n| NSRDB PSM v4 (NLR, formerly NREL) | hourly air temperature and humidity 2021–2025, TMY | October 2026 |\n| TWDB Submitted Driller's Reports and Groundwater Database | driller lithology logs, water levels, aquifer, GCD | 3 October 2026 nightly |\n| USDA NRCS SSURGO (Soil Data Access) | soil texture, density, moisture, restrictive layers | October 2026 |\n\nEvery raw download is cached before it is transformed, so a run can be reproduced from the cache.\n\n## 5. EIA ↔ ERCOT crosswalk\n\nERCOT names resources by substation and unit, not by plant. Each pass/review plant was matched to its ERCOT resource(s) by county, a fuzzy name score (stopwords and Roman numerals normalised), MW and in-service year, choosing the unit subset whose MW best matches the EIA plant. **All 78 candidate plants were verified by hand** (owner, 2–4 October 2026) and the result is kept in `data/crosswalk_eia_ercot.csv`. Two plants have no ERCOT resource (behind the meter) and eleven small T3 plants are not telemetered in SCED; they carry `metrics_status` accordingly and never receive zeros.\n\n## 6. Market metrics (ERCOT, ~31 full months)\n\nThe window runs from the ERCOT public API floor (11 December 2023) to the latest disclosed day (today − 62 days); a month counts only if at least 90% of its days have data. Definitions, per 15-minute interval and summed over the window:\n\n- **Curtailment** = Σ max(HSL − base point, 0) ÷ Σ HSL — the share of available output dispatched down.\n- **Capture rate (delivered)** = generation-weighted node price ÷ time-average hub price (HB_HUBAVG); split into a shape term (solar-hour discount) and a basis term (location discount).\n- **Capture rate (potential)** = the same with HSL (available output) as the weight. Curtailment removes output in negative-price hours, which *flatters* delivered capture for heavily curtailed plants; the potential measure is the one scored.\n- **SCED capacity factor** — net and potential; cross-checked against EIA-923 (fleet medians 22.8% vs 23.7%).\n- **Recent output ceiling** — peak HSL over the last three full months ÷ nameplate; below 0.75 is flagged as a possible derate (a price signal, not a defect).\n\n## 7. Geo layers\n\nEach layer has its own script, raw cache and database table, and is measured from the array polygon (or, for plants without one, a buffer around the EIA coordinate, with confidence lowered).\n\n| Layer | Method | Scored | Confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Transmission | distance to the nearest 345 kV substation; voltages within 3 mi; does a line at the EIA POI voltage run within 3 mi | 345 kV distance (D) | medium |\n| Parcels | host parcels = overlap ≥ 1 acre or 2% of the array; same-owner touching parcels added breadth-first; areas from geometry | acres/MW, headroom, land control (C) | high / medium / low |\n| Gas pipelines | distance to in-service gas transmission (RRC) | no | medium |\n| Flood | share of the array in FEMA SFHA, floodway, 0.2% and Zone D; counties without a digital map are *unknown*, never \"no risk\" | no (flag at ≥ 10% SFHA) | medium |\n| Fiber (proxy) | distance to Class I mainline or interstate right-of-way (where long-haul fiber is laid); great-circle × 1.5 to the nearest carrier hotel for latency | lateral cost in F | low, always |\n| Climate | NSRDB hourly 2021–2025: hours below 25 °C dry-bulb (5-year mean, normalised to 8,760 h); also wet-bulb hours, hours above 35 °C, 0.4% design temperatures, TMY | hours below 25 °C (E) | medium |\n| Wells | share of TWDB driller logs within 3 mi (6, then 10 mi if fewer than 5) with ≥ 20 ft of caliche, gypsum or anhydrite in the top 500 ft; median static water level | drillability, depth to water (E) | medium / low |\n| Soils | SSURGO thermal conductivity, Côté & Konrad (2005) at field capacity, depth-weighted over 0–2 m; restrictive layers | no — informative (owner decision) | medium / low |\n\n**Known biases.** NSRDB temperature is MERRA-2 reanalysis and reads about 1.5–2 °C warm against station normals in Pecos County; it understates cool hours, so the climate points are conservative (accepted by the owner). Driller logs are free text; caliche on the High Plains is drillable with air rotary — the drillability score follows the brief's refusal-layer criterion and is a screen. SSURGO describes the top 2 m only, while a loop bore runs ~150 m, mostly through rock.\n\n## 8. Scoring model\n\nScores are stored as components, section sub-scores (A–F) and a total, so the web app can re-weight them. The positive maximum is **94 points** (the brief's 100, less 6 for soil λ, which is informative only); Section F is a penalty of up to 15.\n\n**A — Acquisition discount (30).** Capture rate (potential), fleet quartiles: ≤ q25 12, ≤ q50 8, ≤ q75 4, else 0 (fleet 0.613 / 0.653 / 0.686). Curtailment, fleet quartiles: ≥ q75 8, ≥ q50 5, else 2 (median 7.3%, q75 12.7%). Quartiles are recomputed at every scoring run over plants with complete market metrics. Capacity factor below the regional benchmark (SCED net CF, else EIA-923; benchmark = regional median: west of −100° 24.0%, coast and south 20.0%, central 21.1%): > 4 points below 6, > 2 below 4, else 1. Offtake status (merchant 4, short PPA 3, long investment-grade PPA 0): no public source, scored *unknown* = 2.\n\n**B — Repowering upside (15).** EIA-860 does not distinguish poly- from mono-crystalline, so module class is a vintage proxy (`module_type_confidence = proxy`): COD ≤ 2018 full points, 2019–2020 half, ≥ 2021 none. Polycrystalline c-Si 6; monofacial 5 (CdTe/CIGS are monofacial and never polycrystalline; a reported bifacial share overrides); fixed tilt or vintage tracker 4.\n\n**C — Physical envelope (20).** Host-parcel acres per MW AC: > 14 → 8, > 10 → 6, > 7 → 3, else 0. Expansion headroom with same-owner land: > 50% → 7, > 20% → 4, else 0. Unified land control (one owner ≥ 90% of host acres) 5.\n\n**D — Electrical (20).** POI voltage: ≥ 345 kV 10, ≥ 138 kV 6, ≥ 69 kV 2, else 0. Distance to a 345 kV substation: < 5 mi 5, < 15 mi 2, else 0 (none within 40 mi is measured data: 0). Load-pocket proximity 5: hand-maintained table pending the PUCT large-load transparency rule; scored neutral.\n\n**E — Thermal & cooling (9).** Drillability (thick-layer share < 10% → 4, < 25% → 3, < 50% → 2, < 75% → 1, else 0; bands confirmed by the owner). Hours below 25 °C: ≥ 6,000 → 3, ≥ 5,000 → 2, ≥ 4,000 → 1. Depth to water: ≥ 200 ft 2, ≥ 100 ft 1. Soil λ (brief bands 2.0 / 1.5 / 1.0 W/m·K for 6 points) is shown but not scored: near-surface estimates cluster at 1.1–1.4 W/m·K, so the bands did not discriminate. A λ below 1.0 sets `thermal_response_test_required`.\n\n**F — Fixed-cost drag (0 to −15).** Fixed costs of $2.4 M (ERCOT study $250k, title and mineral $150k, substation modification $1.5 M, legal and transaction $500k) plus the fiber lateral at $100k per mile, divided by firm IT kW: < $100/kW 0, < $250 −5, < $450 −10, else −15.\n\n**Missing data.** An input with no data, or whose layer is not built, scores half its points (neutral) and lowers `data_completeness`, the share of the positive points that rest on real data; `missing_inputs` names them. A measured \"none within radius\" is data, not missing. Columns ending `_confidence` or `_source`, the flood flag, gas, climate extras and soil λ are displayed, never scored. Review plants are scored but not ranked. As of 6 October 2026 the completeness median is 90.4% (load pocket and offtake remain neutral).\n\n## 9. Caveats on every site\n\n1. **Load import capability is not screenable from public data.** A generation interconnection agreement confers export rights only; import at the POI requires an ERCOT screening study.\n2. **SB6 large-load risk.** Senate Bill 6 (June 2025) set new interconnection requirements for large loads and lets ERCOT curtail them before and during grid emergencies. A material underwriting variable.\n3. **Mineral estate.** The mineral estate is dominant in Texas; severed minerals without a recorded surface waiver keep implied surface access. A deal-killer in the Permian that only a title chain can clear — required on every West Texas site.\n4. **Behind-the-meter configuration is unsettled.** Metering and registration rules for load behind an existing generator are complex and may change under SB6; verify current ERCOT protocols at the time of use.\n5. **Chapter 313 / JETI abatement.** Abatement status and expiry are not screened; the step-up at expiry is often mismodelled.\n6. **Tax-equity consent** is likely needed for plants with COD in 2019 or later.\n7. **ITC recapture** may apply where COD is within the last five years.\n\n## 10. Known data constraints\n\n- ERCOT public API history starts 11 December 2023: the market window is ~31 full months, not 36, and is stored per plant.\n- EIA-923 is monthly for a sample of plants and annual for the rest; `cf_series_resolution` records which.\n- ≥ 10 MW AC does not guarantee SCED telemetry; `sced_coverage` records what the disclosure returned.\n- Long-haul fiber routes are not public: the fiber layer is a corridor proxy (`fiber_confidence = low`).\n- ERCOT's large-load queue is not a downloadable dataset until the PUCT transparency rule lands; the load-pocket input is pending.\n- 34 of the 76 screened plants (6.2 GW, including the whole Permian) sit in counties with no digital FEMA flood map: flood risk there is unknown, not absent.\n\n## 11. Reproducibility\n\nThe pipeline (Python 3.11) runs phase by phase: ingest → plant master → crosswalk and market metrics → geo layers → scores, each phase reading the previous phase's Parquet and writing its own, with every raw download cached. Results load into the database behind the web app; every row carries the `run_id` of the run that wrote it. The XLSX export (`ercot_pv_conversion_screen.xlsx`) contains every computed metric for every pass and review plant, the monthly ERCOT history and the run identifiers.\n";
